{
  "an6.29:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 6.29 ",
  "an6.29:0.2": "3. Unsurpassable ",
  "an6.29:0.3": "With Udāyī ",
  "an6.29:1.1": "Then the Buddha said to Udāyī, ",
  "an6.29:1.2": "“Udāyī, how many topics for recollection are there?” ",
  "an6.29:1.3": "When he said this, Udāyī kept silent. ",
  "an6.29:1.4": "And a second time … ",
  "an6.29:1.5": "",
  "an6.29:1.6": "",
  "an6.29:1.7": "and a third time, the Buddha said to him, ",
  "an6.29:1.8": "“Udāyī, how many topics for recollection are there?” ",
  "an6.29:1.9": "And a second time and a third time Udāyī kept silent. ",
  "an6.29:2.1": "Then Venerable Ānanda said to Venerable Udāyī, ",
  "an6.29:2.2": "“Reverend Udāyī, the Teacher is addressing you.” ",
  "an6.29:2.3": "“Reverend Ānanda, I hear the Buddha. ",
  "an6.29:2.4": "It’s when a mendicant recollects many kinds of past lives. That is: one, two, three, four, five, ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, a hundred, a thousand, a hundred thousand rebirths; many eons of the world contracting, many eons of the world expanding, many eons of the world contracting and expanding. They remember: ‘There, I was named this, my clan was that, I looked like this, and that was my food. This was how I felt pleasure and pain, and that was how my life ended. When I passed away from that place I was reborn somewhere else. There, too, I was named this, my clan was that, I looked like this, and that was my food. This was how I felt pleasure and pain, and that was how my life ended. W